(Clinical Reference: Diabetes Mellitus & Glycemic Markers) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(1. Overview of Glycemic Control and Evaluation) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Diabetes mellitus is a metabolic disorder characterized by persistent hyperglycemia.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Assessment relies primarily on Glycated Hemoglobin \(HbA1c\) and blood glucose metrics.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Proper interpretation requires evaluating both acute and long-term glycemic markers.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(2. Glycated Hemoglobin \(HbA1c\) Reference Intervals) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Normal / Non-diabetic range: Below 5.7%) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Prediabetes \(Increased risk for diabetes\): 5.7% to 6.4%) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Diabetes diagnostic threshold: 6.5% or greater on two separate occasions.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Target for most non-pregnant adults with diabetes: Generally below 7.0%.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(HbA1c reflects average circulating plasma glucose concentration over 8 to 12 weeks.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(3. Plasma Glucose Testing Thresholds) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Fasting Blood Glucose \(FPG\):) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(- Normal: 70 to 99 mg/dL \(3.9 to 5.5 mmol/L\)) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(- Impaired Fasting Glucose \(Prediabetes\): 100 to 125 mg/dL \(5.6 to 6.9 mmol/L\)) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(- Diabetic Fasting Threshold: 126 mg/dL or higher \(>= 7.0 mmol/L\)) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Random Blood Glucose: 200 mg/dL or higher accompanied by symptoms \(polyuria, polydipsia\).) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(4. Clinical Interpretation and Recommendations) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Elevated HbA1c or FPG readings warrant comprehensive clinical evaluation.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Factors affecting HbA1c accuracy include hemoglobinopathies, severe anemia, and chronic renal disease.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Patients should discuss lifestyle, diet, exercise, and potential pharmacotherapy with their physician.) Tj
T*
T*
